BACKGROUND:

The SEQUENCE clause is non-standard SQL syntax to support gap-filling. 
It is expedient rather than elegant.  The same thing can be accomplished
by generating a relation to represent the sequence to fill to and outer
joining it to the input relation.  The issues with this approach are (1)
it's hard to code this approach correctly, (2) the performance of the
result is not good, and (3) most uses require subsequent interpolation.

The SEQUENCE clause addresses these issues by (1) simplifying coding,
(2) using the sort implicit in the windowing framework to advantage, and
(3) realizing the result as a "windowed query" which is immediately
amenable to interpolation.

There are restrictions that may be relaxed over time, however, it
unlikely that the SEQUENCE clause will ever be allowed in conjunction
with either non-sequence windowing or grouping.  (Non-sequence windowing
leads to some semantic ambiguity; grouping would need to occur after
sequencing, opposite to ordinary windowing.  It is best to avoid these
issues.)

APPROACH:

The Window node is extended to support gap filling.  There are two new
variants of the Window node: sequence producer Window nodes and sequence
consumer Window nodes.

The sequence producer form of the Window node produces a gap-filled
sequence of rows.  It must include a sequence definition and supports
only two specific window functions: sequence_key and sequence_index.  A
sequence definition includes exactly one ascending ORDER BY attribute --
the ordering key of the sequence.

The sequence_key window function takes the ordering key attribute and
heeds only it's data type.  It returns the value of the lower bound of
the gap-filled interval for the output row.  The sequence key will be
non-null in case of a "real" row, or null in case of a "filled" row. 
This lower bound is equivalent to the result of the interval_bound
function called with values appropriate to the interval definition.

The sequence_index window function takes no arguments and returns the
number of "real" rows in the sequence to date including the current row.
 It, thus, identifies which "real" row produced the current row in the
sequence.  The result is 0 for leading rows that correspond to no input
row (if any).

The user may, but generally doesn't, call the sequence_key and
sequence_index functions.  The sequence key is available under a
user-specified name as an ordinary attribute.  The sequence index is
used internally.  Both are added as junk attributes unless they are
explicitly called by the user.

The sequence consumer form of the Window node consumes a gap-filled
sequence of rows.  It differs from an ordinary Window node in that it
applies "sequence semantics" in determining peer group breaks.  (An
ordinary Window node defines a peer group as a set of rows within a
partition that share a value of the window's ordering key.  A sequence
consumer Window node defines a peer group as a set of rows within a
partition that "come from" the same input "real" input row, or that
precede the first "real" row in the partition.)

Analysis renders a SQL query with SEQUENCE syntax into an outer window
query marked as a sequence consumer with a subquery marked as a sequence
producer.  Since the output of the sequence producer isn't sorted like
the input, analysis derives the correct output ordering and uses it as
the consumer ordering key.

Planning is sensitive to these producer and consumer window queries in a
few places, but for the most part treats all window queries alike.

As an optimization, the planner avoids introducing an extraneous sort
since, by design, the input to the outer window query is already
correctly distributed and sorted.  In the legacy planner, this
optimization amounts to adjusting the path keys and flow of the output
of the producer query from their original (partition-key, order-key) to
(partition-key, sequence-index, sequence-key, order-key).  As a result,
the requirements of the outer query will be satisfied by its input and
no extra sort will be added.


OUTLINE OF PROCESSING:

User enters a SELECT statement with a SEQUENCE clause in it.  The code
path has a lot in common with the path of a statement with A WINDOW
clause.  (This is mentioned in the following, but mostly we're just
describing sequences.  The outline below proceeds through parse,
analysis, planning, and execution.)

PARSE:

The grammar (gram.y) has some new terms: sequence_clause, sequence_name,
sequence_or_window_clause, sequence_clause_spec,
opt_sequence_partition_clause, sequence_partition_clause,
sequence_order_clause, sequence_definition, and sequence_generator.  In
the definition of simple_select, sequence_or_window_clause replaces
window_clause.  This way the grammar enforces the fact that WINDOW and
SEQUENCE may not occur in the same query block.

Parse trees are modified in that the new structure SequenceSpec (which
points to its associated substructure SequenceDefn) may appear as the
first element of the windowClause of a SelectStmt.  The function
getSequenceSpec, given a list such as the windowClause field, return a
pointer to the SequenceSpec or NULL, if none exists.

ANALYSIS:

Early in analysis (see transformSelectStmt in analyze.c), window or
sequence clauses in the select statement are placed in ParseState member
p_win_clauses.  (Not copied, pointer shared.)  In the case of a
sequence, there is just one.

Soon analysis calls transformWindowSpecExprs which calls either
transformSequenceSpec or, in a loop, transformWindowSpec.  These, in
turn, transform expressions in any clauses found in p_win_clauses in the
ParseState.  When transformWindowSpecExprs is called, early, only the
contents of the WINDOW or SEQUENCE clause are in p_win_clauses.

There is a little rule checking done here, but just within spec, not
between them.  For example, transformSequenceSpec insists that the
supplied SequenceSpec contains an ORDER BY clause with exactly one
ordering attribute.

Importantly, transformSequenceSpec saves the name of the sequence, the
name of the sequence key, and the transformed sequence key and sequence
index expression in the ParseState for use elsewhere, especially in
transformColumnRef (parse_expr.c) and transformTargetListSequenced
(parse_agg.c).

Analysis transforms the central clauses adding them to the Query node
under construction.  (This probably includes calls to transformColumnRef
which handles substitution of the sequence key expression for the
sequence key name and ParseFuncOrColumn in parse_func.c which appends a
pointer for each distinct window function OVER clause content it sees. 
A side-effect of this processing is that WindowSpec nodes are appended
to p_win_clauses and the corresponding WindowRef nodes are marked to
index them.  So p_win_clauses ends up containing the input window clause
and any over clauses encountered during analysis.  If there was a
sequence clause, it will still be first in the list and, since every
window function that uses the sequence will use it by name, it will be
referenced directly.  Uses of PARTITION BY in window function calls are
constrained to "over partition" and "over all" and do not use the
sequence name.

Analysis then calls transformSequenceClause (for window queries,
transformWindowClause) to do the bulk of the checking and setup for the
sequence.  Rules enforced here include no mixture of sequence and
incompatible window stuff.  (Above, window function calls may have been
added to the user specified list of windows; this is the final check.) 
The windowClause list for the Query node under construction is set up to
contain (0) the SequenceSpec, (1) a window spec for an unordered version
of the sequence, if needed by some WindowRef, and (2) a window spec for
"over all", if needed by some WindowRef.

Quite late, analysis calls transformTargetListSequenced to add a junk
attribute for the sequence key, if no user-specified entry for the key
exists.

Finally, analysis calls tranformSequencedWindowQuery which takes
advantage of all the set up to split the input query into a consumer and
producer window Query node.

So the Query tree has the following characteristics:

If it represents a sequence producer query, then the windowClause list
will begin with a SequenceSpec node; the rest (if any) will be
compatible WindowSpec nodes that might be referenced by WindowRef nodes.
 One of the others will refer to the sequence.  Otherwise, if the
windowClause list is non-empty, it will contain only WindowSpec nodes. 
The producer aims to do gap filling.  The only possible window function
calls in the producer are sequence_key(order-key) over (sequence) and
sequence_index() over (sequence).

If it represents a sequence consumer query, then the windowClause list
will begin with a SeqWindowSpec which is just a retyped WindowSpec node
used to communicate use information from the analyzer to the planner. 
The consumer will not contain calls to sequence_key or sequence_index
since these will be delegated to the producer. Otherwise, the
windowClause will be empty or contain only WindowSpec nodes as before
the addition of sequencing.

If it represents a sequence query, then there will be an attributes in
the target list (possibly junk) whose expressions are WindowRef nodes
calling the sequence_key window function (on the ordering key) and
sequence_index window function.  The entries are aliased to the name of
the sequence key specified in the SEQUENCE clause and "sequence_index".

PLANNING:

Planning is mostly ordinary window planning however the planner is
sensitive to the fact that the query a sequence producer or a sequence
consumer.

Function window_planner examines the input Query tree early.

If it is a sequence producer, it sets the WindowContext node's sequence
field to the SequenceSpec and its semantics field to
WINDOW_SEMANTICS_STD.  It also adjusts the result order of the node in
the plan to reflect the result order for the producer as stored (in the
SequenceSpec node's postorder field) by the analyzer.

If it is a sequence consumer, it sets the WindowContext node's sequence
field to NULL and its semantics field to WINDOW_SEMANTICS_SEQ.

Otherwise, it sets these fields to NULL and WINDOW_SEMANTICS_STD.

This conditions Window node construction so that sequence information is
saved in the Window node, and thus communicated to the executor.

EXECUTION:

Functions in nodeWindow.c execute the Window node.  ExecInitWindow sets
up the WindowState.  For producers, it places a pointer to a new
GeneratedSequence based on the input SequenceDefn (from the sequence
field of Window) into the generate_seq_state field and initializes
expressions for comparison and extrapolation of the order key to
sequence key and for sequence execution management into fields of
WindowState.

During execution of a producer the window framework calls the new
function, next_input_row, instead ExecProcNode.  When operating on a
sequence, next_input_row manages the GeneratedSequence, introduces
synthetic rows to fill gaps, and puts the sequence key value, as a
Datum, into the level's sequence_key_ptr field for use by the
sequence_key window function.   This function is what actually puts the
sequence key value into the result of the window node (if it appears in
the target list).

Note that function next_input_row must predict the behavior of the
window framework with respect to partition and sequence key breaks. 
This is so it can manufacture rows (interpolated or extrapolated) that
will make each partition dense in the sequence key.

The datatype specific implementation routines of the overloaded window
function sequence_key(...) issue an error, if they are called outside
the sequenced window framework.  (This should be impossible, though,
since they are defined with an internal type argument.)  They look
inside the internal argument's referent (a WindowRefExprState) and track
back to the per-level state in which the executor has placed the result
Datum for the sequence key.  They return this as the appropriate type.

Execution of a consumer differs from execution of a normal window only
in peer group semantics as already described.
